From ea603c277d55489b955457cca5e27a2b2e33ea84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Woodhouse <dwmw@amazon.co.uk>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:21:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wireguard: socket: fix sticky source address on p2p
 interfaces
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WireGuard attempts to reply from the address to which the original
packet was sent, caching it in endpoint->src4 and validating it before
each use with inet_confirm_addr(..., RT_SCOPE_HOST).

For Legacy IP that validation always fails for an address on a
point-to-point interface, because confirm_addr_indev() insists on
inet_ifa_match(), which compares against ifa_address — the *peer*
address on a p2p link — under a /32 mask. The local address is never
within that "subnet", so a perfectly valid source address is discarded
and send4() falls back to kernel source address selection.

For peers on the far side of the p2p link, the fallback happens to
select the same address and nobody notices. But when a peer on a local
LAN sends to the p2p address (e.g. my OpenWrt box listening on its
external PPPoE address), the reply is sent from the LAN interface's
own address instead. A stateful firewall on the peer then drops the
reply as unrelated to the flow it originated, and the handshake never
completes.

inet_confirm_addr() is an ARP helper answering "do I have an address on
the same subnet as this"; what send4() wants to ask is only "is this
still a local address". Use __ip_dev_find() for that, matching what
send6() already does with ipv6_chk_addr(). IPv6 does not have this
problem.

Fixes: e7096c131e51 ("net: WireGuard secure network tunnel")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: David Woodhouse <dwmw@amazon.co.uk>
Signed-off-by: Jason A. Donenfeld <Jason@zx2c4.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireguard/socket.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireguard/socket.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireguard/socket.c
@@ -50,8 +50,8 @@ static int send4(struct wg_device *wg, s
 
 	if (!rt) {
 		security_sk_classify_flow(sock, flowi4_to_flowi_common(&fl));
-		if (unlikely(!inet_confirm_addr(sock_net(sock), NULL, 0,
-						fl.saddr, RT_SCOPE_HOST))) {
+		if (unlikely(fl.saddr &&
+			     !__ip_dev_find(sock_net(sock), fl.saddr, false))) {
 			endpoint->src4.s_addr = 0;
 			endpoint->src_if4 = 0;
 			fl.saddr = 0;
